{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 restricts 'objectively verifiable' outcomes to non-linear synthesis and declares the panel less reliable on that basis, but the problem never narrows verifiable outcomes to complex, non-linear tasks. For linearly verifiable tasks the panel following an explicit checklist can be more reliable than a single expert, so the blanket claim is unsupported by the problem as stated.","target":"8fd1c920603f60ac7b7cb24528b2e1c47b91f4035c4df86fa114547728506246"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 treats adherence to 'negative-constraint' trap items as the evidence distinguishing reliability, but that only shows the panel resists intuitive pressure; it does not establish that the panel is more reliable than a single expert on average. A panel could pass trap items yet still err on aggregate verifiable outcomes, so the proposed evidence does not answer the problem's requested distinction.","target":"c25161a427244f2495679f29502fb1beaa453e1a5f856471db6fb6ad68a5f220"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 provides no claim or substantive content for assessment, so it fails to argue for or against the required claim about panel reliability versus a single expert and does not identify evidence distinguishing genuine reliability gain from checklist compliance, as the problem demands.","target":"ea67a1be457302fb9b81e85a776e76c62bb30405beee34a7854fe97aba1a85db"}]}